    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d947">

                <pb facs="0846" n="730" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg730"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.112 R 56/11.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams.—Paraphrases.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-03-01T18:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 1, 1912—6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Nine American citizens, including G. P. Robinson, wife and son, and one British
                    subject, fled before threatened assaults at the <pb facs="0847" n="731"
                        xml:id="pg731"/> Suriana mine in the State of Guerrero, took refuge in
                    Telolcapan and urgently requested aid. I made urgent personal representations to
                    the Department of Gobernacion, which sent instructions to General Vego to
                    furnish an escort. British Minister has made similar representations. I will
                    also make usual representations to Foreign Office.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
